\f2 \cf0 20 years Before The Dawkins 
\f1 \
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\pardeftab720\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f4\fs26 	Jamison scurried out of the laboratory, headed straight to the security manager\'92s office. Hayden Miles, Level 3 Manager (Security) quite literally fell off his chair when the richest and most powerful man in the Dominion strolled, alone, into his office. Miles was a typical hardworking family man who loved his job. His proudest moment was being baptized \'93L3\'94 by his delighted wife following his promotion to Level 3 Manger (Security). He loved introducing himself as L3, a meme that quickly took off among the management classes.\
\'93Get up, Mr Miles, we have a bio-security breach.\'94 Jamison announced. \'93Labs 3 through 10 must be quarantined immediately.\'94\
\'93Call me L.3, sir. Full barrier closure?\'94 Miles L.3 asked confidently.\
\'93Yes, doors sealed shut, concrete poured.\'94 CEO Hardy sounded grave and took a deep breath. \'93There can be no leak, we could lose everything.\'94\
L.3. was, by this time, on his feet and already making a vid-call. \'93Yeah, John. Could you execute a \'93Secure and Smother\'94 on Labs 3 through 10?\'94\
\'93No problems.\'94 Said the disembodied head. \'93We can seal the doors now and pour concrete when it arrives.\'94\
L.3. Miles looked to the great Jamison Hardy. \'93All done. The steel blast doors are sealed and cannot be opened from either side. The concrete can be delivered within hours, but it would be better at night, if you want to keep it quiet.\'94\
\'93No, get it done immediately, L.3. I\'92ll worry about damage control.\'94 He knew a night delivery was a much better idea, but time was a factor and a CEO doesn\'92t take suggestions from a Security Manager, no matter how good the notion. He was, however, generous enough to offer some support, \'93Good work, Miles.\'94\
\'93Thank you, Mr Hardy.\'94 
\f5\i By the rites of tax avoidance!
\f4\i0  
\f5\i Jamison Hardy in my office
\f4\i0 . L.3. Miles was star-struck and was kicking himself he had not asked for a selfie. His wife would never believe Jamison Hardy had appeared unannounced in his office.\
Returning to his headquarters, the great Jamison Hardy gathered together his best up and coming accountants, finance guys and economists in his private conference pavilion, the Blue Sky room, high on the top of Hardy Tower One. The soaring steel and glass erection was one of many such projects, scattered across the dominion, commissioned by Jamison to provide low-cost accommodation to the bulging lower classes. He did, however, reserve for himself this one tower, 200 levels of self-indulgence, decreeing a stately glass dome to adorn the summit. Forty-seven metres in breadth, the breathtaking hemisphere of curved glass, set with thick laminated lead crystal, was centred high above the conference hall and allowed strange diffuse light to safely enter, filtering out colossal quantities of radiation the planet no longer deflected. The nature of the highly filtered light, violet and indigo now cast aside, contrived with the curvature of the dome to focus, reflect and refract the remaining beams until they took on a pink and green diffuse MDMA hue, swirling and dancing to a haphazard, silent melody. \
The flickering aurora awed first time visitors into a daze of open-mouthed silence, eliciting cautious inspection of the cavernous glowing pavilion, each step in the dimness judiciously measured. Acclimatisation varied from person to person, but only ten minutes elapsed when Jamison\'92s panel of accountants and finance advisers were seated and ready to hear the great man speak. He sat silent for an age, carefully considering each man and woman seated before him. Satisfied they were sufficiently overwhelmed by his presence he swiped a holograph icon, triggering a burst of bright white light, killing every shadow and instantly painting the giant conference room stark alabaster.\
The participants of this unusual gathering, all now furiously blinking and rubbing their eyes, were seated at two long, thin plasti-fibre tables placed parallel and facing off across 2 metres of gleaming hardwood floor. At one end of the configuration the tables curve desperately toward either side of Jamison\'92s separate oak wood desk, stopping close to the antique edge, daring not to touch divinity.\
\'93Good afternoon ladies and gentlemen. Thank you all for coming on short notice.\'94 Jamison was curt. The assembly of proto-bootlickers all replied, at once, with variations of \'93Of course, Mr Hardy\'94 and deteriorated into a stomach churning cacophony of sucking-up. \'93Okay. Thanks for the support. I don\'92t know any of your names and before you start blurting out the noise your parents used to distinguish you from your over-indulged siblings, I don\'92t want to know. You have been brought here precisely because I don\'92t know you.\'94 Jamison stood up and began to pace slowly and deliberately, commencing a circuit of the tables, contemplating something of gravity. \'93I want a report on the effect to the economy if a drug was created curing all diseases, sicknesses, ailments, illnesses\'85\'85you get what I mean. A world without the need for doctors or medicine. I want to see some blue-sky solutioneering. I want a comprehensive report with conclusions for positive action. I want it in 7 hours.\'94 \
The deadline was set purely by the length of time it took to knock up the special concrete mix required to safely smother a leak of weapons grade bio-agents. Jamison reasoned that he would probably need to pour, but he at least wanted to see if anyone could construct a viable argument supporting the notion that a world without disease was a good thing. \
\'93Well you seem to appreciate the gravity of the situation.\'94 Mr Hardy ordered. \'93You are to remain in this room until the task is complete. No messages - in or out. You will have access to any data-base you think fit and order any refreshments you like, just as long as you complete the task. I will not be happy if the task is incomplete.\'94 He left it at that, knowing no-one wanted an unhappy Jamison Hardy, the consequences were just too grave. Not one of the bright minds sitting before him was brave enough to break the silence. Each mutely contemplating the horror of a world without sick people to suck dry. Jamison sprightly walked towards a blank wall and as he approached a door appeared and creaked open, swallowed him up, before closing and melting back into the wall, leaving no sign.\
\'93All of our careers ride on this ladies and gentlemen. Let\'92s get to it. I suggest we compile a report from the perspective of our particular fields and merge it into one report. Then throw it into cyber-editor to highlight any conflicting ideas and merge writing styles. Then make our recommendations. Any objections?\'94 No-one replied to the self-nominated leader and they all got to it. \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl360\slmult1\sa160\qc\partightenfactor0

