\f4\fs26 One copy hard copy of the report was printed and delivered to Jamison Hardy by the hand of one of his most trusted friends, Germaine Lanning. All electronic records were deleted, the hardware irradiated and shredded. \
\'93Why all the cloak and dagger, big man?\'94 Germaine was one of the few allowed the luxury of talking plain to the wealthiest man in Ulyssia.\
\'93I can\'92t trust the link anymore. There are too many ways for it to be compromised. Snail mail is superior if you can trust the courier.\'94 Jamison hoped he did not have to explain himself any further. He opened the envelope, noting the seal seemed intact and went straight to the conclusions and recommendations, he had no intention of reading all that financial double speak. \
\'93You can trust me, you know that, Jamison.\'94 Germaine slid her hand onto his upper thigh, trying to drag his attention away from the document.\
\'93What? Yes, of course I trust you.\'94 \
\'93I\'92d pour the concrete if I were you.\'94 She laughed, her hand now high enough on his inner thigh as to be regarded as groin. \
\'93Reading my mail?\'94\
\'93Of course. Someone has to take care of you.\'94 She kissed him lightly on the cheek.\
 He studied the final page of the document. \'93Yes, it is just as I thought.\'94 He kissed her in return, this time on the lips. \'93I need you to go back to the authors of this little gem and tell them I am very impressed and let them go home.\'94 \
\'93Of course, Mr Hardy, but don\'92t think this little conversation is over.\'94 They kissed again, long and passionately. She stood and quickly exited, high heels clicking on the hardwood surface.\
Jamison waved through a holographic icon floating above the emitter on his desk. The icon quickly disappeared and was replaced with a male head wearing a security guard\'92s cap. \'93Germaine will be visiting the panel in the Blue-sky room. Please lock the door behind her and open the dome.\'94\
\'93Boiling them inside out? Consider it done.\'94 He opened the dome flooding the Blue Sky Room with radiation and transforming it into a giant microwave. \'93Haven\'92t had a cook-up in a while, sir.\'94\
\'93Have them removed and disposed of. Replace the air and clear the room of radiation.\'94 \
He terminated the call before the guard could reply and set to making another call. \'93Miles L.3.?\'94\
\'93Yes sir. The concrete has arrived and we are ready for your instructions, Mr Hardy.\'94\
\'93Pour it. Shut the labs down.\'94 He knew, as the words were spoken, he was condemning scores of highly educated and hard-working scientists to suffocation. He was especially sad for his loyal employee and good friend Professor Herman. \'93Good-bye Professor.\'94 \
He estimated hundreds of work-years of research would be lost forever. The amount of money he was tossing away did not bare thinking about, however, the report was unequivocal - the economy could not suffer the shock of a healthy population.Hundreds of thousands, perhaps millions would suddenly be out of a job. Treatment of chronically ill patients (the best kind) would dry up quickly and medicines would be worthless. Pharma-stocks would free-fall and the domino effect through other industries was, according to his little panel of experts, was nothing short of catastrophic. They only made one recommendation \'96 \
